    "body": "Problem:\r\nEvery item replaces from compl_col and is filtered by the text it inserts. LSP items do neither: a replace range only has to contain the cursor, so it can start before the word, and filterText is its own field.\r\n\r\nvim.lsp.completion puts the whole batch at the smallest start column any item asked for, pads the rest out to it, then picks one string per item to be both insert text and filter text. Pick wrong and the item drops out of the menu as you type. An item with no range doesn't count towards that column at all, so one overlapping what you typed goes in twice.\r\n\r\nSolution:\r\nAdd \"startcol\" and \"filter_text\" to complete-items. Each item is filtered from its own column, so the client can stop moving everything onto one column and stop guessing. The replacement still ends at the cursor.\r\n\r\nThe spec says as much: \"If the text edit is a replace edit then the range denotes the word used for filtering. If the replace changes the text it most likely makes sense to specify a filter text to be used.\"\r\n\r\nOnly the start of the range is taken. An InsertReplaceEdit starts its insert and replace ranges at the same position, so the start is the same either way; the end is not.\r\n\r\nThis also opens the door for moving \"preinsert\" to Lua with extmarks.\r\n\r\nRelated #30905 #39001 #32142 #15784 #30332 #29287",
    "closed_at": null,
