        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Yeah, mixed start columns is exactly what this is for. Call `complete()` at the normal word start (after the `.`), leave the items that start there alone, and give the ones that reach back a `startcol` plus a `filter_text` that starts with what they replace, since that's what they're filtered against as you type. No need to put the whole batch on one column anymore, which is what eats the text now.\r\n\r\nWith just this PR you'd build that `filter_text` yourself, the replaced text plus the label\r\n\r\n```lua\r\nlocal word = item.text or item.label\r\nif not item.start then\r\n  return { word = word, abbr = item.label }\r\nend\r\n-- `start` is 1-based with columnsStartAt1, adjust if you don't set it\r\nlocal s = vim.str_byteindex(line, 'utf-16', item.start - 1, false)\r\nlocal e = vim.str_byteindex(line, 'utf-16', item.start - 1 + (item.length or 0), false)\r\nreturn {\r\n  word = word,\r\n  abbr = item.label,\r\n  startcol = s + 1,\r\n  filter_text = line:sub(s + 1, e) .. item.label,\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nThat's only for now though. The follow-up rewrites `vim.lsp.completion` on top of these two fields and does this internally: an item whose range reaches back gets its `startcol`, and what it replaces goes in front of its `filterText`. The plan after that is completion sources that hand over LSP-shaped items in-process, so a DAP source would just give `[index]` a range over the `.` (DAP's `start`/`length` are UTF-16 already, like LSP positions) and never deal with `startcol` or `filter_text`.",
